From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: post the nav cluster's keys with E0, from the host
 app's table

winios_drv_post_key took a posted key's scan code from
MapVirtualKeyEx(MAPVK_VK_TO_VSC_EX) and marked it extended when that
gave 0xE0xx. Wine's kbdus table lists the arrows, Insert, Delete, Home,
End, Page Up, Page Down and Print Screen at the numpad's (and SysRq's)
scan codes before its E0 table, so the map answers 0x48 for VK_UP and
the key went out unextended: a game reading scan codes (raw input,
DirectInput) saw numpad 8/2/4/6 for the arrows, from a hardware
keyboard and from a mapped button alike.

The driver now asks the host's playport_key_scan (HostIO hio_keys.c)
first: 0xE0xx for the keys a real keyboard sends with E0 and whose
scan code is the same on every layout (arrows, the nav cluster, Print
Screen as E0 37, right Ctrl and Alt, the Windows and menu keys, keypad
/), 0 otherwise, when Wine's map answers as before. The reference is
weak: a host that does not define it is not called.

Linear: PLA-340
Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-31-extended-keys.md (host test app/tests/keys_test.c)
Offered-upstream: no; the key path is the iOS driver's
---
 build/win32u-unix/driver_ios.c | 18 ++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/build/win32u-unix/driver_ios.c b/build/win32u-unix/driver_ios.c
index cbb42af..b61041b 100644
--- a/build/win32u-unix/driver_ios.c
+++ b/build/win32u-unix/driver_ios.c
@@ -96,6 +96,10 @@ void winios_drv_post_mouse(int x, int y, unsigned int flags, unsigned int mouse_
     }
 }
 
+/* Playport: the host app's scan code for the keys a real keyboard sends with
+ * E0 (its HostIO hio_keys.c). Weak: a host without it is not called. */
+extern unsigned int playport_key_scan( unsigned int vk ) __attribute__((weak));
+
 /* Keyboard sibling of winios_drv_post_key: packages an INPUT_KEYBOARD
  * event. vk is a Windows virtual-key code (VK_RETURN=0x0D, VK_SPACE=0x20,
  * VK_ESCAPE=0x1B, ...); flags is 0 for key-down, KEYEVENTF_KEYUP (0x2)
@@ -124,11 +128,17 @@ void winios_drv_post_key(unsigned short vk, unsigned int flags)
      * W/A/S/D were delivered, accepted with STATUS_SUCCESS, and then discarded
      * as unidentifiable. That is why the on-screen stick moved nothing.
      *
-     * MAPVK_VK_TO_VSC_EX returns 0xE0xx for the extended keys — arrows, the nav
-     * cluster, right ctrl/alt, numpad enter and divide. Those MUST carry
+     * The extended keys — arrows, the nav cluster, Print Screen, right
+     * ctrl/alt, the Windows keys, numpad divide — MUST carry
      * KEYEVENTF_EXTENDEDKEY, or a scan-code reader sees the numpad twin
-     * instead: without E0, "up arrow" is numpad 8. */
-    scan = NtUserMapVirtualKeyEx( vk, MAPVK_VK_TO_VSC_EX, NtUserGetKeyboardLayout(0) );
+     * instead: without E0, "up arrow" is numpad 8. MAPVK_VK_TO_VSC_EX does
+     * not give them all: Wine's kbdus table lists the arrows, the nav cluster
+     * and Print Screen at the numpad's (and SysRq's) scan codes first, so it
+     * answers 0x48 for VK_UP. Playport: the host app's fixed table answers
+     * for these first (their scan codes are the same on every layout); every
+     * other key keeps Wine's map. */
+    scan = playport_key_scan ? playport_key_scan( vk ) : 0;
+    if (!scan) scan = NtUserMapVirtualKeyEx( vk, MAPVK_VK_TO_VSC_EX, NtUserGetKeyboardLayout(0) );
     if (scan & 0xe000) flags |= KEYEVENTF_EXTENDEDKEY;
 
     input.type           = INPUT_KEYBOARD;
